.\" Manual page for portkiller.
.TH PORTKILLER 1 "2026" "portkiller" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
portkiller \- interactive terminal port manager for Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B portkiller
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B portkiller
shows who is listening on which ports, enriched with process, project,
health and kill-impact information, and stops listeners safely: host
processes get SIGTERM (then SIGKILL after 3 seconds with
.BR --force ),
containers are stopped with docker stop or podman stop (rootless podman
containers as their owner), and systemd services through
systemctl. A PID is only signalled while its kernel start time still
matches the scan, so a recycled PID cannot be killed by mistake.
Running with no flags starts the interactive TUI.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-list " [" \-\-json " | " \-\-plain ]
Print a table of all listeners; \-\-json emits machine-readable JSON and
\-\-plain tab-separated fields (proto, address, port, pid, project,
process, source; "\-" for an empty field).
.TP
.BI \-\-kill " PORT..."
Stop everything listening on the given port(s). Multiple ports may be
given in one command. A container is stopped via \fIdocker stop\fP or
\fIpodman stop\fP, a
systemd unit via \fIsystemctl\fP, and a host process receives SIGTERM.
Retry with \fI--force\fP to escalate to SIGKILL if the PID is still
alive after 3 seconds.
A listener run by a process manager (pm2, supervisord, nodemon, forever,
runit, s6) gets a warning on stderr naming the command that stops it for
good, since the manager may start it again.
.TP
.BI \-\-kill\-name " NAME..."
Stop every listener whose process name, or container name, is
\fINAME\fP. May be combined with \fI--kill\fP.
.TP
.BI \-\-signal " SIG"
With \fI--kill\fP or \fI--kill-name\fP: send the one signal \fISIG\fP
(HUP, INT, QUIT, KILL, USR1, USR2, TERM, STOP, CONT, or a number) instead
of SIGTERM-then-SIGKILL. Containers get it through \fIdocker kill\fP or
\fIpodman kill\fP, systemd units through \fIsystemctl kill\fP. Cannot be
combined with \fI--force\fP.
.TP
.BR \-\-wait " [\\fISECONDS\\fP]"
After stopping, wait until the stopped ports have no listener (10 seconds
unless given) and exit 1 if one is still taken.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
With \fI--kill\fP: print one line per listener showing what would be
stopped and how, without touching anything.
.TP
.BR \-\-free " \fISTART\-END\fP [\\fI\-\-json\fP]"
Print the first bindable TCP port in the range.
.TP
.BR \-\-history " [\\fI\-\-port\fP N] [\\fI\-\-limit\fP N] [\-\-json]"
Print recent appear/vanished events from the events log, newest first.
.TP
.BI \-\-completions " SHELL"
Print a completion script for \fIbash\fP, \fIzsh\fP or \fIfish\fP.
.TP
.BR \ \-\-help ", " \-\-version
Show help or version information. These run without sudo.
.SH EXIT CODES
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Nothing stopped, at least one stop failed, a requested port or name has
no listener, or a port was still taken after \fI--wait\fP.
.TP
.B 2
Bad or unknown argument.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR ~/.config/portkiller/guard.conf
Guard rules, one \fIport=owner\fP per line.
.TP
.IR ~/.local/state/portkiller/events.jsonl
History log of port appear/vanish events.
.TP
.IR ~/.config/portkiller/hidden
Process names hidden from the TUI table (H toggles, U shows them).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ss (8),
.BR docker (1),
.BR podman (1),
.BR systemctl (1)
